-- strictApply (runtime/prelude.c, backing seq/$!/$!!/$##) called
-- force(x) once and then read x's tag/nondet directly, without ever
-- chasing FORWARD_TAG -- forward_hnf is a deliberate no-op, so if x was
-- already a forward wrapper (e.g. the result of some earlier collapse
-- point that wasn't safe to copy -- extremely common, since choose()
-- itself always produces one) when strictApply ran, force(x) never even
-- reached the real value, and seq silently failed to notice a failure
-- reached that way.
--
-- `failed ? 99` gets collapsed to a forward by the enclosing let (a
-- fresh choice node is never collapse_safe), so seq's own argument is
-- that forward, not the raw choice. On the first exploration (side =
-- failed), a *correct* implementation detects the failure and silently
-- skips it (only "SOLUTION: 1" from the second alternative, side = 99,
-- should ever print).
--
-- Confirmed on a scratch build reverted to the pre-fix strictApply:
-- SOLUTION: 1 (bogus, from the unnoticed failed alternative)
-- SOLUTION: 1 (the real one, from side = 99)
-- -- two identical lines, the first one wrong. Fixed:
-- SOLUTION: 1 (just the real one).
--
-- A second, related gap fixed alongside the FORWARD_TAG chase: even
-- once x is properly chased to the real, failing node, that node's own
-- `nondet` flag was never itself touched by choose() -- only the choice
-- node's was -- so a naive "chase, then check x.n->nondet" fix still
-- under-reports nondet and would leave root's own fail(root) untrailed.
-- Needs accumulating nondet at every hop of the chase, exactly like a
-- generated case-dispatch loop's own `nondet |= _scrutinee.n->nondet`.
main :: Int
main = (failed ? 99) `seq` 1
